# ===========================================================================
# v1.0.61 -- THE RE-CUT AFTER v1.0.60 WAS WALKED AND FAILED
# ===========================================================================
# v1.0.60 is not a version that went unexamined. It was BUILT, PUBLISHED and
# WALKED, the walk FAILED, and Andy called it: v1.0.60 does not ship. That is
# the system working -- a walk is the only rung that can return this answer,
# and it did. v1.0.61 exists to carry the same window plus what the walk cost
# us, not to retry the same bytes and hope.
#
# SCOPE IS NINE MERGES, all CM051 install.sh or its tests, each one
# mutation-verified against the real pre-fix tree before it merged. They are
# named in the WHY THESE PINS block at the foot of this file rather than here,
# so this header states the DECISIONS and the pin block states the CONTENTS.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS CUT REBUILDS NO DAEMON, AND THE ARGUMENT IS RECORDED ON PURPOSE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DAEMON_COMMIT stays at bc63c445 (hub-v0.4.70). oa main HAS moved -- to
# 4ebf1e1f -- so the naive read is "the pin is stale, rebuild". It is wrong
# here, and the discriminating question is not "did the SHA move" but "did
# anything that REACHES THE ARTEFACT move":
#
#     compare bc63c445...main   ahead_by 1, behind_by 0, total_commits 1
#     4ebf1e1f                  1 file, .gitignore, +16 -0
#     outside .gitignore        0        <- nothing here can reach the binary
#     CONTROL, same predicate on bc63c445 itself   1   (so the 0 is real)
#     oa Quality Gate @ bc63c445   14 check-runs, all completed/success,
#                                  enumerated 14 == declared total_count 14
#
# A pin reused WITHOUT this argument is indistinguishable from a pin nobody
# checked, which is why it is written down and not merely acted on.
#
# ⚠️ BOUNDARY ON THAT CLAIM, because it decides who to believe if they
# disagree: the above measures the DELTA, not the artefact. Nobody has
# verified that bc63c445's BUILT BINARY is still the one the manifest hashes.
# That is check-freshness's job -- the gate that killed v1.0.55 -- and if it
# reds it beats every line above it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PIN MUST NAME A COMMIT REACHABLE FROM main -- STANDING, NOT HISTORICAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Carried forward from v1.0.60 because it is a rule, not a war story: a pin
# that resolves in your clone and over `gh api` can still be absent on CI,
# because a squash-merged branch's own commits are unreachable from every ref
# and `actions/checkout` fetches all REACHABLE history, not all objects. Two
# instruments agreeing is not evidence when both measure something CI does not
# have. The discriminator is `git merge-base --is-ancestor <pin> origin/main`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS CUT HAS NO WALK RECORD WHEN IT IS MADE. THAT IS CORRECT, NOT A GAP.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# walks/v1.0.61.tsv does not exist yet, so verify_walk_record.sh returns rc=2 --
# absence, correctly NOT conflated with badness -- PROMOTE=0, the release stays
# a prerelease, and ostler.ai/install.dmg keeps resolving to its current
# target. The sequence is cut -> walk -> record -> only then promote.
#
# ⚠️ PUT THE DMG IN THE WALKED ACCOUNT'S ~/Downloads. post_walk_qa.sh looks
# only in $HOME/Downloads, $HOME/Desktop and $HOME of the account it walks
# as, and on a CLEAN record the gate then REQUIRES artefact_sha256_source to
# read measured(...). A clean walk whose DMG sits elsewhere yields PROMOTE=0
# for a reason that reads as "no walk evidence" rather than "wrong folder".
#
# 🎩 WALK SUBJECT: NOT YET NAMED. Archie sets it. It is deliberately blank
# rather than inherited -- the previous header named a specific machine and an
# address, and copying that forward would assert a subject nobody has chosen
# for this cut. An unnamed subject is honest; a wrong one is worse than none.
#
# 📌 THIS HEADER WAS WRITTEN FRESH, AND THE PREVIOUS ONE DID NOT ARRIVE HERE
# BY ACCIDENT. scripts/new_cut.sh copies cut.env and rewrites exactly three
# assignment lines, "anchored to line start so a mention inside a comment is
# never rewritten -- the comments are the record". So every prior narrative
# rides forward verbatim unless a human rewrites it, and v1.0.61 came out of
# the generator still titled v1.0.59. Structural, not a slip: see CM051 #960.

CUT_VERSION=1.0.66
DAEMON_COMMIT=bc63c445
# 🔴 REPINNED 2026-09-02. THE PREVIOUS PIN WAS UNRESOLVABLE ON CI, AND IT
# PASSED LOCALLY, WHICH IS THE WHOLE LESSON. 203e19e4 was the head of a
# feature branch that was SQUASH-merged and deleted. A squash writes a NEW
# commit, so the branch's own commits become unreachable from every ref --
# and `actions/checkout` with fetch-depth: 0 fetches ALL REACHABLE history,
# not all objects. CI therefore had no such commit and the gate correctly
# returned CANNOT-RUN rather than pretending to agree.
#
# ⚠️ IT RESOLVED EVERYWHERE I LOOKED FIRST, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVED. My
# clone still had the object because the branch had been checked out here,
# and `gh api .../commits/203e19e4` ALSO returns it, because GitHub keeps
# unreachable objects addressable over the API. Two instruments agreed and
# both were measuring something CI does not have. The discriminator is
# `git merge-base --is-ancestor <pin> origin/main`, which answers NO.
#
# THE PIN MUST NAME A COMMIT REACHABLE FROM main. This one is main's head at
# the time of writing, and the gate is not weakened by that: it compares
# install.sh BLOBS, and 00440f72's blob is 8a2c094e -- byte-identical to
# 203e19e4's. Nothing about the tree being cut changed; only the name used
# to reach it did.
CM051=b0a11f4d

